<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="monolignol biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-361" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: The expected taxonomic range of the pathway includes Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The phenylpropanoids are a large family of compounds, mostly found in plants, that are synthesized from |FRAME: PHE| and, to a lesser extent, |FRAME: TYR|. The pathway that starts from |FRAME: PHE| forms |FRAME: COUMARATE| as a main intermediate. The name &quot;phenylpropanoid&quot; is derived from this compound, which consists of an aromatic phenyl group and a propene tail. Further processing results in the biosynthesis of many types of compounds, including |FRAME: Lignols monolignols| (precursors to |FRAME: Lignins lignin| and lignocellulose), |FRAME: Flavonoids flavonoids|, |FRAME: Coumarins coumarins|, |FRAME: Stilbenoids stilbenoids|, and phenolic acids. Phenylpropanoids are found throughout the plant kingdom, where they serve as essential components of a number of structural polymers, provide protection from ultraviolet light, defend against herbivores and pathogens, and also mediate plant-pollinator interactions as floral pigments and scent compounds. |FRAME: Lignins Lignin| is a three dimensional polymer of phenylpropanoid alcohols (monolignols). Lignin is always associated with cell wall |FRAME: CELLULOSE| and |FRAME: Hemicelluloses|, and provides mechanical rigidity to plant-supporting and plant-conducting tissues. There are three monolignols, |FRAME: COUMARYL-ALCOHOL|, |FRAME: CONIFERYL-ALCOHOL|, and |FRAME: SINAPYL-ALCOHOL|. The relative amounts of these monolignols found in lignin show plant specificity. Gymnosperm lignins contain mainly coniferyl alcohol, angiosperm lignins contain both coniferyl alcohol and sinapyl alcohol, whereas all three types of monolignols are found within lignins of grasses. The transport of monolignols to the cell wall and their lignification to lignin is poorly understood. These events possibly involve glucosylation. Lignification is possibly catalyzed by peroxidase. In the past decade, the lignin biosynthesis pathway has been redrawn several times based on new genetic and biochemical evidences. The pathway depicted here only illustrates the major routes to lignin monomers.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="PMN" />
    <Author name="nilo-poyanco" />
  </Authors>
